%I A401189 #15 Oct 19 2026 10:05:02
%S A401189 4,12,20,28,44,52,60,68,76,84,92,116,124,132,140,148,156,164,172,188,
%T A401189 204,212,220,228,236,244,260,268,276,284,292,308,316,332,340,348,356,
%U A401189 364,372,380,388,404,412,420,428,436,444,452,460,476,492,508,516,524,532,548
%N A401189 Nonsquarefree numbers k that divide A400873(k).
%C A401189 First differs from its subsequence A081770 at n = 67: a(67) = 648 is not a term in A081770.
%C A401189 The relative asymptotic density of A081770 within this sequence is 1/(Pi^2*d) = 0.994473..., where d is the asymptotic density of this sequence that is mentioned below.
%C A401189 Differs from A364999 by having the terms 4, 648, 3240, 4536, 5184, 7128, 8424, 11016, 11025, 12312, ... .
%C A401189 If k is squarefree then A400873(k) = k.
%C A401189 Numbers k such that A400873(k)/k is an integer > 1.
%C A401189 Analogous to e-perfect numbers (A054979), with the sum of exponential divisors of the exponential divisors (A400873) instead of the sum of exponential divisors (A051377). e-multiperfect numbers k for which A051377(k)/k is an integer larger than 2 are not known.
%C A401189 The corresponding quotients are 2, 2, 2, ... . a(1125) = A401190(4) = 11025 is the least term with a quotient larger than 2 (see A401190 for other possible quotients).
%C A401189 If k and m are coprime terms, then k*m is also a term, since A400873(n)/n is multiplicative.
%C A401189 If m is a term, and k is a squarefree number coprime to m, then k*m is also a term.
%C A401189 The primitive terms in this sequence are the powerful terms (A401190). This sequence can be generated from the primitive terms by multiplying with coprime squarefree numbers.
%C A401189 The asymptotic density of this sequence is Sum_{n>=1} f(A401190(n)) = 0.1018842344424344..., where  f(n) = (6/(Pi^2*n)) * Product_{prime p|n} (p/(p+1)).
%H A401189 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A401189/b401189.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%t A401189 f[p_, e_] := DivisorSum[e, DivisorSigma[0, #]*p^(e/#) &]; q[k_] := Module[{r = (Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[k])/k}, r > 1 && IntegerQ[r]]; q[1] = False; Select[Range[550], q]
%o A401189 (PARI) isok(k) = {(my(f = factor(k), v = prod(i = 1, #f~, sumdiv(f[i, 2], d, numdiv(d) * f[i, 1]^(f[i, 2]/d))))); v > k && !(v % k);}
%Y A401189 Subsequence of A013929.
%Y A401189 Subsequences: A081770, A401190.
%Y A401189 Cf. A005117, A051377, A054979, A364999, A400873.
%Y A401189 Analogous sequences: A068978 (with all the divisors), A054862 (unitary), A401188 (infinitary).
%K A401189 nonn,easy,new
%O A401189 1,1
%A A401189 _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 17 2026
